WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A park sign must have a matching design on the opposite side
of a mirror line.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.960 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sliding a copy across does not always produce the right
result.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.040 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How can we place each matching point exactly?

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.995 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we will use the mirror line and equal distances to
construct reflections.

00:00:16.995 --> 00:00:17.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to reflect points and shapes using a
mirror line.

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:28.755 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:28.755 --> 00:00:30.115 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:30.115 --> 00:00:35.692 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:35.692 --> 00:00:39.532 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to reflect points and shapes using a
mirror line.

00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:42.332 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:42.332 --> 00:00:49.733 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:52.413 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:52.413 --> 00:00:54.373 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can locate the mirror line.

00:00:54.373 --> 00:00:57.333 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can match equal perpendicular distances.

00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:59.493 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can check the reflected shape.

00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:03.333 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:12.058 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:12.058 --> 00:01:19.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw a simple shape on paper, fold the page along a straight
line and imagine transferring the marks to the other half.

00:01:19.018 --> 00:01:22.418 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When opened, matching points lie on opposite sides of the
fold.

00:01:22.418 --> 00:01:28.430 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The fold gives the mirror line, which controls the position
of every reflected point.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:32.750 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our first mirror is vertical, passing through horizontal
coordinate five.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.830 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We can describe it as the line x equals five.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:39.150 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every point on that line has first coordinate five.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:45.281 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The mirror is a whole line, not a single point to rotate
around or a boundary that moves with the shape.

00:01:45.281 --> 00:01:49.121 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a vertical mirror, measure horizontally from the point
to the line.

00:01:49.121 --> 00:01:52.081 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That direction is perpendicular to the mirror.

00:01:52.081 --> 00:01:52.401 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place the image the same distance on the opposite side.

00:01:52.401 --> 00:01:57.681 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The height stays unchanged.

00:01:57.681 --> 00:02:03.678 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Measuring diagonally would give the wrong matching position
even if the diagonal lengths happened to agree.

00:02:03.678 --> 00:02:05.598 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Point A is at two, three.

00:02:05.598 --> 00:02:08.558 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The mirror is the vertical line through five.

00:02:08.558 --> 00:02:11.198 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many units separate A from the mirror?

00:02:11.198 --> 00:02:17.638 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the image coordinates by placing its partner the
same distance on the other side, at the same height.

00:02:17.638 --> 00:02:20.431 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do not simply swap the coordinate numbers.

00:02:25.431 --> 00:02:27.591 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A is three units left of the mirror.

00:02:27.591 --> 00:02:29.991 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its image must be three units right of the mirror, at eight,
three.

00:02:29.991 --> 00:02:30.111 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two points share the same height.

00:02:30.111 --> 00:02:39.816 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their joining segment crosses the mirror at a right angle,
and the mirror cuts that segment into two equal lengths.

00:02:39.816 --> 00:02:41.416 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our triangle also has B at four, three and C at two, five.

00:02:41.416 --> 00:02:42.536 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
B lies one unit left of the mirror, so its image is six,
three.

00:02:42.536 --> 00:02:51.496 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
C lies three units left, so its image is eight, five.

00:02:51.496 --> 00:02:55.809 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Join the reflected corners in matching order to complete the
image.

00:03:09.929 --> 00:03:10.049 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A and B do not travel the same horizontal distance between
original and image.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:10.849 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A crosses six units, while B crosses two.

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:11.049 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their distances depend on how far they start from the
mirror.

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:13.516 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Applying one shared slide would be a translation, and would
not produce this reflected triangle.

00:03:13.516 --> 00:03:16.876 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point on the mirror line is zero units away from it.

00:03:16.876 --> 00:03:20.236 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its reflected position is therefore the same point.

00:03:20.236 --> 00:03:20.836 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This is a useful check when a shape touches or crosses the
mirror.

00:03:20.836 --> 00:03:28.857 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection does not force every point to move to a
different location.

00:03:28.857 --> 00:03:31.817 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now use a horizontal mirror at height four.

00:03:31.817 --> 00:03:33.737 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Point A is at two, one.

00:03:33.737 --> 00:03:38.937 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict its image by measuring straight upward to the mirror
and the same distance beyond it.

00:03:38.937 --> 00:03:43.378 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which coordinate should stay unchanged this time, and which
one must change?

00:03:48.378 --> 00:03:50.098 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The point is three units below the mirror, so its image is
three units above, at two, seven.

00:03:50.098 --> 00:03:53.578 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The first coordinate stays two.

00:03:53.578 --> 00:03:55.538 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The vertical distances to height four are equal.

00:03:55.538 --> 00:04:04.995 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The same distance rule works for either orientation of
mirror; only the perpendicular direction changes.

00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:10.315 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A reflection preserves side lengths and angles, so the image
is the same size and shape.

00:04:10.315 --> 00:04:14.355 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Its orientation is reversed, like writing seen in a mirror.

00:04:14.355 --> 00:04:18.675 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare matching corners rather than deciding only from the
outline.

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:23.661 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Symmetric shapes can conceal the reversal, making point
checks especially useful.

00:04:23.661 --> 00:04:27.341 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect the image across the same mirror a second time.

00:04:27.341 --> 00:04:33.861 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each point returns to its original location because the same
equal distance rule acts in reverse.

00:04:33.861 --> 00:04:37.301 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This provides a practical way to check your construction.

00:04:37.301 --> 00:04:42.167 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If a point does not return, inspect the mirror position and
its distance first.

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:46.727 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When comparing the original and image, keep the mirror line
in the same place.

00:04:46.727 --> 00:04:50.607 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Moving the line halfway through creates a different
transformation.

00:04:50.607 --> 00:04:53.927 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Label the line clearly and draw the matching point pairs.

00:04:53.927 --> 00:04:58.794 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That makes your reasoning visible to someone who did not
watch you construct the image.

00:04:58.794 --> 00:05:02.714 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point at four, three reflects in the vertical line through
five.

00:05:02.714 --> 00:05:05.594 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A student puts its image at seven, three.

00:05:05.594 --> 00:05:08.794 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is the new point the correct distance from the mirror?

00:05:08.794 --> 00:05:14.735 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare the original and proposed distances before deciding
where the image really belongs.

00:05:19.735 --> 00:05:22.855 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The original point is one unit left of the mirror.

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.975 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Seven, three is two units right, so the distances do not
match.

00:05:24.975 --> 00:05:29.855 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The correct image is six, three, one unit right.

00:05:29.855 --> 00:05:35.657 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A clear distance check explains exactly why the first
proposal failed and why the repair works.

00:05:35.657 --> 00:05:37.497 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:37.497 --> 00:05:40.457 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect points and shapes using a mirror line.

00:05:40.457 --> 00:05:45.457 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:45.457 --> 00:05:50.097 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:50.097 --> 00:05:56.645 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
